WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.600
A useful review skill is learning to recognize risky API patterns before focusing on any single function name.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:12.340
Missing size arguments are one of the clearest warning signs when a function writes into caller-provided memory.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.160
If the callee cannot know the destination capacity, safety depends entirely on assumptions outside the call.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.180
Fixed-size buffers deserve the same scrutiny.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:27.880
A fixed buffer can be perfectly acceptable when the maximum input size is enforced before the copy or conversion occurs.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:35.460
It becomes risky when the maximum is only implied by a comment, guessed from a protocol, or assumed because current test data is small.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.640
Unchecked return values are another recurring problem.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:48.300
Many APIs communicate truncation, failed conversion, short reads, incomplete writes, allocation failure, or invalid input through their return value.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:55.240
If the caller ignores that signal, the next line of code may operate on incomplete or invalid data while appearing to succeed.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:00.420
Implicit null-termination assumptions can turn bounded data into unsafe string handling.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:07.920
A buffer read from a file, socket, environment entry, or binary record may have a known byte count without being a valid C string.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:15.100
Passing that buffer to string-only routines before establishing termination and length can create unreliable behavior.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.000
Ownership and lifetime rules also belong in the contract.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.740
Who owns the pointer?

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.000
Who frees it?

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.220
How long is the returned value valid?

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.280
Can it point to internal storage?

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.020
Can the caller modify it?

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:35.200
Ambiguity here can lead to leaks, stale pointers, double cleanup, or use of data after its valid lifetime.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:40.860
Code review should identify the contract around each pointer, buffer, length, and returned value.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:47.000
The safest call sites make capacity, used length, ownership, and error behavior visible.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:52.980
When those details are hidden, the first task is to reveal the contract before choosing a replacement API.
